### Java Code

```java
package com.example;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.channels.SocketChannel;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class ConnectionManager {

    private static final String REMOTE_ADDRESS = "localhost";
    private static final int PORT = 8080;
    private static AtomicInteger connectionAttempts = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public static void manageConnection() {
        SocketChannel socketChannel = null;
        try {
            socketChannel = SocketChannel.open();
            socketChannel.configureBlocking(false);
            boolean connected = socketChannel.connect(new InetSocketAddress(REMOTE_ADDRESS, PORT)).isDone();

            if (connected) {
                System.out.println("Connected to server.");
            } else {
                System.out.println("Connection attempt: " + connectionAttempts.incrementAndGet());
                manageTimeout(socketChannel);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error establishing connection: " + e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            if (socketChannel != null) {
                try {
                    socketChannel.close();
                    sendMessageOnClose();
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    System.err.println("Error closing the channel: " + e.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private static void manageTimeout(SocketChannel socketChannel) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        int attempts = 0;
        while (!socketChannel.finishConnect() && attempts < 5) {
            Thread.sleep(1000);
            attempts++;
        }

        if (attempts == 5) {
            System.out.println("Connection timed out.");
        }
    }

    private static void sendMessageOnClose() {
        // Simulate sending a message to a messaging system
        System.out.println("Connection closed. Message sent to the system.");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        manageConnection();
    }
}
```

### Explanation

- **C Code**: Manages memory allocation for multiple data structures, checks against a memory limit, and handles resource cleanup.
- **Java Code**: Establishes a non-blocking connection using `SocketChannel`, manages timeouts, and sends a message upon disconnection.

This solution includes error handling and resource management to prevent segmentation faults and memory leaks. It also provides a mechanism to log and manage connections in Java, with the C code integrating JNI calls to interact with the Java component.']

